Keep the EditContext buffer where the keyboard expects it after its own edit.

NativeEditContext gives the browser's EditContext the text of the lines the
selection is on, with offsets counted from column 1 of the first of them, and
moves that window to the caret's line whenever the caret changes lines. An
input method keeps its own copy of the text and acts on it before it hears
what an edit did, so a move it did not ask for leaves it working on offsets
that now point somewhere else.

Gboard 12.4 does exactly that with Enter. With a word underlined and the caret
inside it or at its start, as after a tap on the word or Home, it sends Enter
as a key press and at once recomposes the word after the caret at the offsets
of its copy: the old line with a line break at the caret. By then the window
had moved to the new line, so the range landed on other characters and the
keyboard wrote the word over them. On an API 33 emulator, in
`const alpha1 = 1, alpha2 = 2, alpha3 = 3;`, a tap inside alpha1 then Enter
left `ha1 = 1, aha1a2 = 2, alpha3 = 3;` on the new line, a tap before it left
`alpha1 alpha1lpha2 = 2`, and Home then Enter left `cconstalpha1 = 1`. The
same Enter at column 1 left the window's text and caret exactly as they were,
so the keyboard was told nothing, kept the line break it believed it had
typed, and spent the next Backspace deleting it: one Backspace did nothing
for every such Enter.

After an edit the keyboard made, a textupdate or its Enter, the window now
keeps its start, so the line break the keyboard typed is in it and the
Backspace it sends for that line break has something to delete. Where text
follows the caret, the window starts instead where the caret sits at the
offset the keyboard expects, the event's own selection for a textupdate and
one past the old caret for the Enter, so that the recomposed range covers the
text after the caret the keyboard believes in. Keeping the start there is not
enough, measured: in indented code the editor puts the indentation after the
line break, the recomposed range then covered three of its four spaces, and
`    const alp|ha1 = 1;` became `ha1 ha1 = 1;`, where the per-line window had
only been saved by its range falling past the end of the line.

The start moves to that offset only where text follows the caret, because
the keyboard's copy of the text before the caret then lacks the indentation
the editor added, and nothing tells the keyboard so. Moved after every edit
of the keyboard's, measured, it broke Enter at the end of an indented line:
told the caret it expected, Gboard kept its copy, took the second Backspace
for the `;`, recomposed what it took for `alpha1` over `a1;`, the line break
and two spaces, and the third Backspace after Enter at the end of
`    let beta2 = alpha1;` left `    let beta2 = alphalpha`, which the per-line
window never did. With the start kept, the indentation puts the caret off
the offset the keyboard expects, and it reads the text again. With text
after the caret, its copy stays short of the indentation until the caret
moves for another reason, so a Backspace right after Enter inside a word on
an indented line deletes one space of the indentation, as the per-line window
did; the Backspaces after it go on taking out the indentation and then the
line break.

Anything else that moves the caret, a tap, a cursor key, a command other
than a Backspace sent as a key press (see below), a paste or an undo, still
moves the window to the caret's line, and the keyboard, told of a caret it
did not expect, reads the text again. Only what the
keyboard does itself keeps an earlier start: a window that kept it across
every edit reached back over lines nothing had typed, and opening a file at
its saved caret on line 3 gave the keyboard lines 1 to 3 as one buffer.

A keyboard is told of a selection that moved and of nothing else: Android's
InputMethodManager passes a selection on only when it differs from the last
one it passed (its updateSelection in the API 33, 36 and 37 sources; the path
through Chromium was not traced), and what was measured matches that. Where
the caret's offset in the new window is the one it had in the old, the
keyboard never hears that the text changed and goes on from the text it had.
On an API 36 emulator with Gboard 18.4,
Enter at the end of `ell`, Ctrl and the space bar, and `d` left the window at
`ell\nd` with the caret at 5; Tab accepted `delta`, the window moved to the
caret's line, `delta` with the caret at 5, and the suggestion strip went on
offering `d`, `do` and `did`, where `do` made the line `deltao `. A tap from
the end of a line holding `delta` to the end of `alpha` in
`alpha beta Charlie delta`, offset 5 in both, left the strip on `delta`, and
its `Delta` replaced `alpha`. That route needs no kept start: the published
bundle also starts the window at the caret's line after a tap, and replayed
in a browser it rewrote the window with no selection update for that tap, for
taps at column 1 of two lines and for Down along a column, where it did
update the selection after the Tab. So on Android, after a rewrite that is
not an edit or a selection of the keyboard's, which its own copy holds
already, what the keyboard knew before the first rewrite of the task is kept,
and once the code that rewrote the window has returned, still in the same
task, input is restarted if the text then differs from it and the selection
does not. The editing host is blurred and focused again with the editor's
focus tracker paused, so that the editor is not told it lost focus, as the
keyboard guard and the key row's latched chord restart input from the page,
and the keyboard reads the text again. Deciding at the end of the task is
what keeps the restart to those cases: accepting a suggestion or a paste
rewrites the window before the caret moves, and decided at that rewrite,
replayed in a browser, a suggestion accepted with the caret moving on
restarted input as well. Waiting also keeps the end of a composition the
blur brings out of the edit, and makes the two rewrites the Tab made restart
input once. It costs a restart at every tap, cursor key or command that
leaves the caret at the same offset of another line, column 1 included, at
every command that rewrites the caret's line without moving the caret, such
as a forward Delete and its undo, and at every edit made after the caret on
its line in a later task, as linked editing makes while a tag is typed, or a
replacement before the caret by text as long; an edit before the caret that
changes its length moves the caret's offset, which the keyboard is told of,
and restarts nothing. On a phone the refocus would put a keyboard that was
up away if the page's keyboard guard wrote `inputmode="none"` on the host at
that focus, as its focus handler did after a drag, a long press or a menu
button outside text: Chromium hides the keyboard when the focused element's
input mode turns to none, unless a hardware keyboard is attached, and the
emulators measured all report one. The guard keeps the
keyboard up across a host blurred and focused again in one task
(MainActivity.injectKeyboardGuard). Text the editor adds inside
an edit of the keyboard's, such as an auto-closed bracket or quote, is not in
the keyboard's copy, and since that rewrite counts as the keyboard's own it
restarts nothing, as nothing told the keyboard of it before (read from the
code, not measured).

A textupdate that changes no text, which is how a selection the keyboard sets
arrives, as Gboard's do for a space bar swipe and before it commits a
suggestion, puts the selection where the keyboard asked, on whichever line of
the window that is, and leaves the window as it is, its end included. A
keyboard goes on from its own copy of the text as if its selection had landed.
Applied like an edit, the update typed the text before the caret again, over
every line the window holds, and moved the caret along its line only, stopping
at column 1. Moving the caret alone, along its line, with the window kept, was
not enough either. On the API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4, after `one`, Enter
and `alph`, a swipe left on the space bar moved the caret to the start of
`alph`, the step across the line break moved nothing, and Gboard, its own
caret at the end of `one`, wrote `one` over `alph` with no further input,
twice out of twice. Told of a caret it did not ask for, that keyboard did not
read the text again. With the selection where it asked, the caret goes on to
the end of `one`, as in a text box. The window then keeps the lines after the
caret's, which the keyboard's copy holds: ended at the caret's line, it would
refuse a swipe back to the right at that line's end in the same way. Those
lines stay across what the keyboard does next, and go, with the window's
earlier start, at the next tap, cursor key or command.

The selection is set as the textarea input sets one the keyboard asks for
(ViewController.setSelection): one selection, from source 'keyboard' with
reason Explicit, as a cursor key moves the caret, so several cursors become
one, as they do there. Moved as compositionType moves it, with the reason
typing has, the move was taken for typing that changed nothing: the suggest
model refilters an open list only for an edit or an Explicit move, and opens
one when typing moves the caret to the end of a word. Replayed the same way,
with a keyboard that commits, `x alp` under a list open on `alpha1` and the
caret moved back after `x` kept the list, and Enter or Tab then made the line
`xalpha1`, where the published bundle, which typed the text again, closed the
list at the move, split the line on Enter and inserted a tab at the caret on
Tab. A move onto the end of a word, after Escape had closed the list, opened
it again, in the published bundle too. With reason Explicit the list is
refiltered at the new caret, closing once the caret leaves the word, and a
quick suggestion still pending is dropped, as patch 0023 drops it for a cursor
key.

A Backspace sent as a key press keeps the window as it is too, from the
press to its release. Gboard 17.7.4 and 18.4.1 send their Backspace that way,
which the editor runs as its deleteLeft command, and count the character in
their own copy of the text, as they count an edit they sent. Reset as after
any other command, the window dropped the lines after the caret's that a
swipe into the line above had kept, while the caret moved to the offset the
keyboard expected, so the keyboard was told nothing and went on from its
copy. On an API 37 emulator with Gboard 17.7.4, after `gamma`, Enter and
`zeta`, a space bar swipe back into `gamma` and a Backspace left the window
at `gama`; the swipe back to the right was refused past its end, the strip
offered words for `zeta`, and the pick of `zebra` deleted the two characters
before the editor's caret and wrote `bra `, which made the first line
`gabra ` and left `zeta` as it was, in three attempts out of four. A
Backspace that deletes more than the one character the keyboard counts, back
to a tab stop of indentation, leaves the caret off the offset the keyboard
expects, which it is told of. With a modifier the key is left to its command
as before. Gboard 12.4 deletes through textupdates, which keep the window
already, and sends a key press only where its copy holds nothing before the
caret; the line join that makes takes the caret before the window's start,
and the window then starts at the caret's line, as before.

All of this is done on Android only, the one platform it was measured on:
elsewhere every edit starts the window at the caret's line, and a selection
is typed as an edit, as before.

The window is built with \n line breaks whatever the model uses, since the
keyboard counts a line break as one character. Typing line after line keeps
its start, so the window grows with each line, until an edit leaves the
caret at the end of its line with more than 20 lines before the caret's: the
window then moves to the caret's line, where nothing follows the caret for
the keyboard to recompose. While text follows the caret, an earlier start is
kept however far back it is.

A Backspace the keyboard sends for a line break before the caret now joins the
lines. CompositionOperation replaced characters before the caret on its own
line only, clamped at column 1, which made that Backspace nothing; it now
reaches back across line breaks, each counting as one character as in the
window. It reaches forward across them in the same way, because the lines the
window keeps after the caret's line are text the keyboard can replace: clamped
at the end of the caret's line, a recomposition of `alph` on the line after
`one`, replayed with the caret at the end of `one`, put a second `alph` in
front of the first. With several cursors the counts apply at each of them, as
they always did, and the walks with them: a recomposition of `bean` around the
primary cursor, two characters on each side, replaced the `yz` before a
secondary cursor at the end of `xyz` and the line break and `p` after it,
leaving `xbeanqrstu` where `xyz` stood over `pqrstu`; the published bundle,
clamped to the secondary cursor's line, left `xbean` and `pqrstu`. The
composition underline and the character bounds map offsets through the
window's own start rather than the selection's line.

Verified on the API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 with the built tree
installed. All three of the flows above left the text intact and a Backspace
after each joined the lines back. The indented one kept `    const alp` over
`    ha1 = 1;`, and five Backspaces after it took out the four spaces of
indentation and then the line break, one each, before an `x` landed at the
caret. Three Enters on an empty last line were undone by three Backspaces.
Enter at the end of `    let beta2 = alpha1;`, six Backspaces and an `x` left
`    let beta2 = alpha1x`, as the published bundle does, and a `}` typed on
the new line, which the editor outdented, then three Backspaces and an `x`
left the same. Typing a word, Enter and four more words left the buffer at
`kiwi \nalpha beta charlie delta`, and a tap and a suggestion from the strip
left it on the caret's line alone. Then the built bundle, driven in headless
Chromium with an Android user agent and CDP IME calls replaying Gboard's
sequence (finish, Enter key, recomposition at the stale offsets), beside the
published 1.139.1 bundle: the old one wrote `aha1a2`, `cconstalpha1` and
`    ha1 = 1;ha1` and its Backspaces after three Enters deleted nothing; this
one kept each line, and its Backspaces joined the lines back. Enter at the end
of the indented line reported the caret at 28 in a buffer from the line's
start, where the keyboard expected 24, and Ctrl+Enter, a command, left the
buffer on the caret's line. With a desktop user agent the window stayed on the
caret's line throughout. Selections replayed the same way, as Blink sends one
the keyboard sets. After `one`, Enter and `alph` composed as Gboard 12.4
composes them, a swipe back five characters put the caret at the end of `one`,
the keyboard's recomposition of `one` there left both lines intact and its `x`
gave `onex`; the bundle that moved the caret along its line only wrote
`oonealph` for the same keyboard, and the sequence Gboard sent on the emulator
after its refused step wrote `one` over `alph`, as it had there. That sequence
recomposes `alph` with the caret at the end of `one`, twice, and then writes
`one` into the composition: this bundle replaced `alph` with itself each time
and then with `one`, which is what that keyboard asked for, where the replace
clamped at the end of the caret's line put each `alph` in front of the first
and left the lines `one`, `one`, `alph`, `alph`, `alph`. Gboard 12.4 sent it
after a refused move; with the move landing, it recomposed `one` on the
emulator. A swipe back to the right went into `alph` again, and an `x` gave
`alxph`; a tap after the swipe left the window on the tapped line alone. A
keyboard that moved back one character at a time over `kiwi `, `one`, `two`
and `alph` took the caret into `two`, both in step at every step. Moves along
a line changed neither the window nor the model, where every one of them had
rewritten the lines before the caret; Gboard's suggestion pick after an Enter,
its selection, a delete before the caret and the commit, still left
`kiwi \nalpha `; and with two cursors the selection left one. With the list
open on `x alp`, a move back after `x` closed it, and Enter split the line and
Tab inserted a tab, as in the published bundle; a move onto the end of `alp`
after Escape opened no list. Every other flow replayed here printed byte for
byte what it printed before the selection crossed lines, apart from the
selections across a line break, which now land. With the kept start read after
the reason test instead, the window went back to the caret's line at each
move, and the correction after a selection left `alpha alphas`. On the
emulator with the tree built just before the replace reached forward, the
swipe after `one`, Enter and `alph` took the caret on into `one`, where Gboard
recomposed `one` with both lines intact, a swipe back to the right took it to
the end of `alph`, and an `x` after a shorter swipe gave `alxph`; by the event
log, no edit the keyboard made there replaced anything past the caret's line.
With a desktop user agent the selections were typed as before, line by line.

The restart was replayed the same way, with a keyboard that commits, in a file
holding `alpha beta Charlie delta`, `ell` and `delta`. In the published bundle
and in this one before the restart, a tap from the end of `delta` to the end
of `alpha`, a tap from column 1 of one line to column 1 of the other, and Down
twice along column 3 each rewrote the buffer with no selection update; Enter
at the end of `ell`, Ctrl and the space bar, `d` and Tab did so here only,
where the published bundle, its buffer `d`, moved the selection. With the
restart each was followed by one blur and focus of the host, after the
rewrite, with the editor's focus state unchanged throughout, while a tap to
another offset, typing with Enter and a commit over a composition, and Tab
accepting `alpha` over `al` restarted nothing; decided at each rewrite instead
of at the end of the task, that last one restarted input as well. With `d`
still composed when Tab accepted `delta`, in a harness with no keyboard guard
to end the composition, a letter composed next landed at the caret, `deltax`,
where without the restart it took the place of the `a` under the composition
range Chromium had kept, `deltx`. Every other flow replayed here printed what
it printed before, apart from a status bar position read at once after typing,
which differed between runs of either bundle. Not measured on a device.

The Backspace key was replayed the same way, from the event log of the API 37
emulator, with a keyboard that commits and sends its Backspace as a key
press: `zeta` typed after Enter at the end of `gamma`, selections 9, 8, 7, 6
and 4, the Backspace, selections 4 and 7, and the pick of `zebra` as a
selection at 9, the two characters before the caret deleted and `bra `
committed. The bundle before this kept the window at `gama` after the
Backspace and wrote `gabra ` over the first line, as on the emulator; this
one kept `gama\nzeta` with the caret at 3, followed the selection into `zeta`
and made the second line `zebra `. A Backspace at the end of the second line
kept the window from the first line, one at the start of the window's only
line joined it to the line above as before, and with a desktop user agent
the window stayed on the caret's line. Every other flow replayed here printed
byte for byte what it printed before. On the API 37 emulator with the built
tree, `gamma`, Enter, `zeta`, a swipe into `gamma`, the Backspace and a swipe
back left the window holding both lines and the caret at the end of `zeta`,
and `zebra` from the strip replaced `zeta` with the first line as the
Backspace had left it.

